    "title": "How Does Prairie View A&M University Rank Among America's Best Colleges?",
    "text": "Prairie View A&M University is a public institution that was founded in 1876. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Prairie View A&M University is ranked No. #343 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #117 in Top Performers on Social Mobility. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 8,836 (fall 2024), its setting is rural, and the campus size is 1,502 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Prairie View A&M University is 16:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Prairie View A&M University accepts the Common Application. The school's in-state tuition and fees are $7,386; out-of-state tuition and fees are $22,590. Eighty-four percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $12,889. The four-year graduation rate is 20%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $36,350.Prairie View A&M University is a public institution that was founded in 1876. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Prairie View A&M University is ranked No. #343 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #117 in Top Performers on Social Mobility. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 8,836 (fall 2024), its setting is rural, and the campus size is 1,502 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Prairie View A&M University is 16:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Prairie View A&M University accepts the Common Application. The school's in-state tuition and fees are $7,386; out-of-state tuition and fees are $22,590. Eighty-four percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $12,889. The four-year graduation rate is 20%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $36,350.",
